1
00:00:00,109 --> 00:00:02,052
CostCook turns a catering order

2
00:00:02,052 --> 00:00:05,666
into your shopping list, prep, and food cost.

3
00:00:05,666 --> 00:00:06,980
Here's a real order.

4
00:00:07,689 --> 00:00:08,898
An order comes in.

5
00:00:08,898 --> 00:00:13,816
A backyard wedding, 200 guests, Aug 15.

6
00:00:13,816 --> 00:00:16,113
I type in the menu, the guest count,

7
00:00:16,113 --> 00:00:19,156
and what I'm charging a head. $13.

8
00:00:19,156 --> 00:00:21,072
That's all CostCook needs.

9
00:00:21,072 --> 00:00:24,194
Everything you'll see comes from this page.

10
00:00:24,957 --> 00:00:27,756
First, the shopping list.

11
00:00:27,756 --> 00:00:32,104
Every dish's ingredients, rolled up

12
00:00:32,104 --> 00:00:35,174
into one list, scaled to 200.

13
00:00:35,174 --> 00:00:38,327
Red onion is in the pilaf and the salad.

14
00:00:38,327 --> 00:00:39,536
Here it's one line.

15
00:00:39,536 --> 00:00:44,020
15.4 lb. One bag, 25 lb.

16
00:00:44,020 --> 00:00:46,085
Grouped by supplier,

17
00:00:46,085 --> 00:00:47,973
in packs you can actually buy.

18
00:00:47,973 --> 00:00:50,541
3 cases of chicken thigh from Sysco.

19
00:00:50,541 --> 00:00:52,905
5 cases of pita from the bakery.

20
00:00:52,905 --> 00:00:57,688
Each stop gets its own subtotal.

21
00:00:57,688 --> 00:01:00,011
And down here, the honest number.

22
00:01:00,011 --> 00:01:02,199
About $900 out the door,

23
00:01:02,199 --> 00:01:05,514
$785 in what the food itself costs.

24
00:01:05,514 --> 00:01:07,321
The gap is pack round-up.

25
00:01:07,321 --> 00:01:09,617
This list used to be my whole evening.

26
00:01:09,617 --> 00:01:11,924
Now it's done before the coffee's poured.

27
00:01:12,646 --> 00:01:14,019
Then the prep list.

28
00:01:14,019 --> 00:01:17,225
What the line actually cooks, after trim.

29
00:01:17,225 --> 00:01:19,997
You buy almost 80 lb of chicken thigh.

30
00:01:19,997 --> 00:01:22,972
59.5 lb goes in the pot.

31
00:01:22,972 --> 00:01:25,119
The loss is already in the math.

32
00:01:25,119 --> 00:01:28,176
The marinade? Six whole batches.

33
00:01:28,176 --> 00:01:30,945
Nobody's doing that arithmetic at 5 a.m.

34
00:01:31,667 --> 00:01:33,909
The pack list is the van check.

35
00:01:33,909 --> 00:01:36,545
Every dish, with its portion count.

36
00:01:36,545 --> 00:01:38,814
Check each one off as it's loaded.

37
00:01:38,814 --> 00:01:41,776
Equipment can ride along here too.

38
00:01:41,776 --> 00:01:45,254
Chafers, sternos, whatever the job needs.

39
00:01:45,254 --> 00:01:47,303
Nothing off-site gets forgotten.

40
00:01:48,012 --> 00:01:50,104
Now the part that protects you.

41
00:01:50,104 --> 00:01:52,400
Every dish knows its plate cost.

42
00:01:52,400 --> 00:01:55,715
These skewers: $1.74 on the plate,

43
00:01:55,715 --> 00:01:57,387
yield loss included.

44
00:01:57,387 --> 00:02:00,960
At $5.50 they run 31.6%.

45
00:02:00,960 --> 00:02:05,743
Over my 28% target, so it's flagged.

46
00:02:05,743 --> 00:02:08,202
The fix: charge $6.22 or better.

47
00:02:08,202 --> 00:02:10,104
Same math for the whole event.

48
00:02:10,104 --> 00:02:16,273
$2,600 in, $785 food, 30.2%.

49
00:02:16,273 --> 00:02:19,017
A dime more a guest hits the target.

50
00:02:19,017 --> 00:02:21,191
I used to find out after the party.

51
00:02:21,191 --> 00:02:22,968
Now I see it before I quote.

52
00:02:23,690 --> 00:02:24,913
That's CostCook.

53
00:02:24,913 --> 00:02:27,508
The order goes in, the lists come out,

54
00:02:27,508 --> 00:02:29,057
and you know your numbers.

55
00:02:29,057 --> 00:02:31,435
costcook.io · Book a demo.

56
00:02:31,435 --> 00:02:34,502
Bring a real order. I'll run it live.

